  "query_preview": "..(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 526 AM EST THU NOV 19 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, the 500 mb pattern will be \ndefined by a deep and broad trough centered over the Intermountain\nWest, with ridging now well east of the forecast area. Meanwhile,\nan additional wave will be located off Florida's Atlantic coast. \nAt the surface, an area of high pressure will be located over the \nNortheast. Low pressure will be centered over Ontario, with a cold\nfront extending to its southwest. Another relatively deep low \nwill be positioned farther south near the Bahamas, although this \nlow now looks to stay well east of the forecast area. Even so, \nmore active weather is expected locally by late Sunday as the cold\nfront approaches.\n\nInitially, winds in the forecast area will be southeasterly, \nlargely directed around the aforementioned surface high. With \ntime, however, the surface high will lift northeast, and while \nwinds should be southeasterly through Saturday, a shift towards \nslightly stronger and more southwesterly surface winds will be \nlikely on Sunday as the Canadian low translates eastward and the \npressure gradient tightens. Early on, downslope oriented \nsoutheasterly winds will support a dry air mass near the surface, \neven while southwesterly flow aloft allows total column moisture \nto increase. Surface dewpoints look to remain in the 30s through \nthe day on Saturday, despite increasing precipitable water. By \nSunday, however, more meaningful (albeit still modest) surface \nmoisture return via southwesterly winds will allow dewpoints to \nclimb into the upper 40s and perhaps the low 50s in the western \nportion of the forecast area. This increased surface moisture, in \naddition to anomalously high PW values (LREF mean PW of 0.7 to 0.9\ninches, representing at least the 80th percentile compared to \nclimatology), will set the stage for eastern Kentucky's first \nwidespread rainfall in some time. Although weather should remain \nclear and dry on Saturday and early Sunday, cloud cover looks to \nincrease during the day on Sunday as the cold front approaches. \nThese initially clear skies will support ridge/valley splits on \nFriday and Saturday nights, but increasing clouds should keep \nSunday night's lows warmer than in previous days, with valleys \nlargely remaining in the mid 40s. High temperatures on Sunday will\nremain dependent on the timing of the front, which has trended \nlater than in previous forecast packages, but as of now, it \nappears most likely that cloud cover could keep maxTs lower than \nin previous days for at least the northern portion of the forecast\narea, although temperatures will still remain above average \noverall through the weekend. \n\nThen, rain chances increase late Sunday into Monday morning, with\nPoPs peaking in the 40-50% range for most of the forecast area. \nEnsemble guidance is on board with this rain, suggesting \nmeasurable precipitation is probable across the forecast area \n(with the LREF suggesting roughly a 90% or greater chance to meet \nthis threshold nearly areawide by Monday evening), although timing\nuncertainties still preclude higher PoPs in the official forecast\nat this time. Despite this, overall QPF looks to be meager. The \nfront should be relatively weak and fast moving, and diurnal \ntiming will not be favorable; currently, rain chances look to peak\nbefore dawn on Monday morning, and with virtually no instability \nexpected, rain rates will be unimpressive at best. Thus, even a \nquarter inch of rain appears unlikely at this time, with QPF more \nin the neighborhood of 0.1 inches. Unfortunately, this will do \nvery little to mitigate moderate (D1) drought conditions in our \nfar southwestern counties. \n\nBehind the front, more northerly to northwesterly winds will lead\nto more seasonable to slightly below average temperatures, with a\ndrier air mass temporarily setting in. Model spread does increase\nsignificantly from Tuesday onwards, with considerable\ndisagreements regarding the evolution of the next trough, although\nit appears likely that another period of more active weather will\nbe possible around midweek. Thus, chance PoPs are in the forecast\non Wednesday for now, although confidence is low and details are\nsparse at this stage in the forecast.",
